<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perugino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1523">1523</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Pietro Perugino, Pietro Vannucci</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Perugino, born Pietro Vannucci, was an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> painter of the Umbrian school who worked mainly in Perugia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. Known for calm, balanced compositions and gentle figures, he painted the Delivery of the Keys in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> and decorated the Collegio del Cambio in Perugia. The young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> trained in his workshop.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perugino.jpg" alt="Perugino" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pietro Perugino · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_Perugino_031.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pietro Vannucci was born in Città della Pieve, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, the son of Cristoforo Maria Vannucci. His nickname marks him as from Perugia, the chief city of Umbria. Scholars dispute the standing of his family: some hold that he worked his way out of poverty, others that the family was among the wealthiest in the town. He most likely began studying painting in local workshops in Perugia, such as those of Bartolomeo Caporali or Fiorenzo di Lorenzo.</p><p>According to Vasari he was apprenticed in the Florentine workshop of Andrea del Verrocchio alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>, Domenico Ghirlandaio and Filippino Lippi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-della-francesca">Piero della Francesca</a> is thought to have taught him perspective. In 1472 he was enrolled as a master in the Confraternity of St Luke. He was one of the earliest central Italian painters to work in oil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Around 1480 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> called him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to paint fresco panels on the walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a>. These included the Baptism of Christ and the Delivery of the Keys; Pinturicchio went with him and became his partner. The frescoes on the altar wall were later destroyed to make room for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>'s Last Judgement. Between 1486 and 1499 he worked mainly between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and Perugia, keeping a studio in each. In 1496 the bankers' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of Perugia asked him to decorate the audience hall of the Collegio del Cambio, with planets and zodiac signs on the vault and sacred scenes on the walls; he placed his own portrait bust on the central pilaster.</p><p>He became one of the priors of Perugia in 1501. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> is said to have called him a bungler in art, and Perugino brought an unsuccessful action for defamation. His Annunziata Altarpiece in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, painted in 1504 to 1507, was accused of lack of innovation, and he lost his students. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> summoned him to paint a ceiling in the Vatican, but soon preferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>, who had been trained by Perugino, and from 1512 Perugino retired to Perugia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His last frescoes were painted in 1521 and 1522 in churches and monasteries around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>. He died of the plague in 1523 at Fontignano and, like other victims, was hastily buried in an unconsecrated field, the spot now unknown. He left considerable property and three sons. Vasari states that he had very little religion and doubted the immortality of the soul. His likely pupils included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>, whose early work shows his influence most, and Perugino's Marriage of the Virgin, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a>, served as the model for Raphael's Sposalizio of 1504. Perugia dedicated a monument to him in 1923.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pietro Perugino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the Life of Christ on the right side and the Life of Moses on the left, painted by Sandro Botticelli, Domenico Ghirlandaio, <b>Pietro Perugino</b>, Pinturicchio, Luca Signorelli and Cosimo Rosselli, while the upper level has the windows and, between them, painted niches with the first 32 popes.</blockquote><a href="/work/sistine-chapel-ceiling">Sistine Chapel ceiling · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Città della Pieve, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Fontignano, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.95,12&amp;year=1497" aria-label="Città della Pieve on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Perugino"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5746 3863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5746 3863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.95,12&amp;year=1497">What was here in 1497? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
